{
  "summary": "A four-year-old boy named Ariel accidentally broke a 3,500-year-old Bronze Age jar at the Hecht Museum in Haifa, Israel, when his mother looked away momentarily. The rare artifact, which had been on display for 35 years and was one of the only complete containers from that period (2200-1500 BC), was likely used for storing wine or oil. Rather than banning the family, the museum welcomed them back a week later, where Ariel presented his own clay vase as a gift and was met with forgiveness from staff.",
  "note": "While this incident disrupted the usual pattern of museum visits, the individual child's accident led to an unexpected outcome. Unlike someone deliberately damaging artifacts after breakfast or knocking items off a chair, this was purely accidental. The museum's forgiving response became a heartwarming song of understanding rather than punishment."
}
```

